      "es": "# Command 1113 - HSM Average CPU Utilization\n\n- **Command ID:** `1113`\n- **Title:** HSM Average CPU Utilization\n- **PDF Pages:** 1338 - 1339\n\n---\n\n\n<!-- Page 1338 -->\n\n1113 - HSM Average CPU Utilization\nCommand 1113 returns a percentage value which is the average CPU utilization for the HSM over a\nprevious time period.\nCommand\n| <1113#Time | Value#[[Measurement |  | Unit#]0#]]> |\nResponse\n\n```\n<2113#Percent Utilized#>[CRLF]\n```\n\n\n### Command description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 4 | 1113 |\n| 1 | Time Value | Variable | The time value depends upon the measurement units |\nspecified in field 2.\nWhen field 2 = 0, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 3599.\nWhen field 2 = 1, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 1439.\nWhen field 2 = 2, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 167.\nWhen field 2 = 3, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 369.\nWhen field 2 = 4, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 52.\nWhen field 2 does not exist, the measurement unit is\nseconds.\n| 2 | [Measurement Unit#] | [1] | This field is optional. When present, it contains a decimal |\ndigit, where:\n0 = seconds\n1 = minutes\n2 = hours\n3 = days\n4 = weeks\n| 3 | [0#] | [1] | This field is optional. This field can be present if field 2 is |\npresent. When present, this field contains the number 0.\n\n### Response description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 4 | 2113 |\n\n\n<!-- Page 1339 -->\n\n\n### Response description (Continued)\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 1 | Percent Utilized | 1 - 3 | This field contains a decimal number in the range of 0 - 100. |\nUsage Notes\n This command is only allowed on the Serial and Management ports.\n The HSM continuously measures CPU utilization. The percent utilized value returned in the response is\nbased on the previous time value. For example, if the measurement is 10 seconds. The HSM returns the\nCPU utilization for the previous 10 seconds.\n The measurement data is stored in volatile memory. Whenever the HSM is restarted the previous\nmeasurement data is lost. You should not define a measurement for a time period which exceeds the\nHSM uptime.\nExamples\nObtain the average CPU utilization for the previous 10 seconds\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<1113#10#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<2113#22#>\n```\n\nObtain the average CPU utilization for the previous 2 hours\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<1113#2#2#0#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<2113#41#>\n```\n\nObtain the average CPU utilization for the previous 8 weeks\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<1113#8#4#0#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<2113#28#>\n```\n\n\n",
      "en": "# Command 1113 - HSM Average CPU Utilization\n\n- **Command ID:** `1113`\n- **Title:** HSM Average CPU Utilization\n- **PDF Pages:** 1338 - 1339\n\n---\n\n\n<!-- Page 1338 -->\n\n1113 - HSM Average CPU Utilization\nCommand 1113 returns a percentage value which is the average CPU utilization for the HSM over a\nprevious time period.\nCommand\n| <1113#Time | Value#[[Measurement |  | Unit#]0#]]> |\nResponse\n\n```\n<2113#Percent Utilized#>[CRLF]\n```\n\n\n### Command description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 4 | 1113 |\n| 1 | Time Value | Variable | The time value depends upon the measurement units |\nspecified in field 2.\nWhen field 2 = 0, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 3599.\nWhen field 2 = 1, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 1439.\nWhen field 2 = 2, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 167.\nWhen field 2 = 3, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 369.\nWhen field 2 = 4, this field contains a value in the range of 1\n- 52.\nWhen field 2 does not exist, the measurement unit is\nseconds.\n| 2 | [Measurement Unit#] | [1] | This field is optional. When present, it contains a decimal |\ndigit, where:\n0 = seconds\n1 = minutes\n2 = hours\n3 = days\n4 = weeks\n| 3 | [0#] | [1] | This field is optional. This field can be present if field 2 is |\npresent. When present, this field contains the number 0.\n\n### Response description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 4 | 2113 |\n\n\n<!-- Page 1339 -->\n\n\n### Response description (Continued)\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 1 | Percent Utilized | 1 - 3 | This field contains a decimal number in the range of 0 - 100. |\nUsage Notes\n This command is only allowed on the Serial and Management ports.\n The HSM continuously measures CPU utilization. The percent utilized value returned in the response is\nbased on the previous time value. For example, if the measurement is 10 seconds. The HSM returns the\nCPU utilization for the previous 10 seconds.\n The measurement data is stored in volatile memory. Whenever the HSM is restarted the previous\nmeasurement data is lost. You should not define a measurement for a time period which exceeds the\nHSM uptime.\nExamples\nObtain the average CPU utilization for the previous 10 seconds\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<1113#10#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<2113#22#>\n```\n\nObtain the average CPU utilization for the previous 2 hours\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<1113#2#2#0#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<2113#41#>\n```\n\nObtain the average CPU utilization for the previous 8 weeks\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<1113#8#4#0#>\n```\n\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<2113#28#>\n```\n\n\n"
