      "es": "# Command 130 - RSA Data Encrypt\n\n- **Command ID:** `130`\n- **Title:** RSA Data Encrypt\n- **PDF Pages:** 802 - 803\n\n---\n\n\n<!-- Page 802 -->\n\n130 - RSA Data Encrypt\nCommand 130 encrypts data using an RSA public key.\nReference Information: Section 9, “Asymmetric Cryptography.”\n| Key Type |  | Default Setting | MFK Type |\nAKB Disabled AES\nCommand\n\n```\n<130#Padding Scheme#Data#Public Key#>\n```\n\nResponse\n\n```\n<230#Encrypted Data#[Check Digits]#>\n```\n\n\n### Command description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 3 | 130 |\n| 1 | Padding Scheme | 1 | Indicates the padding algorithm used with RSA encryption: |\n0 = OAEP-SHA256 padding (both hashes = SHA256)\n2 = OAEP-SHA1 padding\n9 = PKCS#1 v1.5 padding\nLegal Characters: 0, 2, 9\n| 2 | Data | Variable | The data to be encrypted. The length of the data must be |\neven. The minimum length is two characters. The\nmaximum length depends on the padding scheme and size\nof the RSA modulus (SOM)\nField 1 Maximum length\n0 2*SOM - 132\n2 2*SOM - 84\n9 2*SOM - 22\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\n| 3 | Public Key | Variable | The Public Key to encrypt the data. |\nHeaders Supported: 3DRE*000, where * = E, N or S.\nWhen field 1 is 0 or 2: 3DRO*000 is also supported.\nThe length of this field depends upon the length of the\nPublic Key. This field can contain a key slot number where\nthe Public Key is stored in the RSA Key Table.\nLegal Characters: Printable ASCII\n\n\n<!-- Page 803 -->\n\n\n### Response description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 2 | 230 |\n| 1 | Encrypted Data | 2*SOM | The encrypted data. |\n| 2 | [Check Digits] | 0, 8 | The check digits of the RSA public key pair. The check |\ndigits are the leftmost 8 hexadecimal characters of the\nSHA256 of the RSA public key\nThis field will contain 8 hexadecimal characters only\nwhen field 3 of the command contains a number in the\nrange of 0 - 199.\nAKB AES MFK Example\nAES Master File Key = 2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF\nFEDCBA9876543210, check digits = B9BCAB7B62, see “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component\nvalues.\nThis command generates data which is returned in the response, your responses will not exactly match the\nresponses shown below.\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<130#0#8F#3DREE000,0001030100D80E983004D54EEF3EF15D103972EB9282BE14CF6CCC44D0A\n```\n\nCED6B6E6A2C9C976582F0870D7AA35C1E1F8DDA8083C7D023A0156E71A388206AADD9F0B536FF4\nE6EF15EE468AFA0AF187791D79EA0A1AF30955735344553888F2DBCB6D13EBEEAF908A199783B5\n72D71C30519F0CCFB3E5735393790C1CAF6ADA6512749F72814718086CE6F151FA9DC77CA2A4F9\nD15726B679D0516F11F30A0E28FAC127747EE8A0686BD5A222DEF36FB38B393DF0E5988F682BFC\n5B6F1682726B623BD7F5AE2B8901D79E356B3CAB9B5936722701A5652B14C16EE4DC750715556A\n87AC4B97E9B266F316A3E9AFDCDFD79C8157C323653F1959BAE851817933D2D3DE26DFCA3,F088\n979B7C9F4AC8B6AA429F4FF48C5C#>\nThe HSM returns a response similar to this:\n\n```\n<230#14815159E757A287867ADF7F1F2161A86F4B39054BDAD687054B762F21BC9BAB097AFDA69\n```\n\n46BBF1C340BB2C989B1B969E623D2FBF87228E69FA317FFB42D5DD64FB794D095554A136DB5DFF\n90D9A13C79FBED8459132A736D2E55D4F9C3BC40CA1DAF54E7816D4D1A2F2742564DB8C76DD2BD\nF5E6BEDAB9FE2C0DF5B93848A7B04DB45D051BE8DF9E4BF1AE96FC30051A5F091D3670E0B86EF7\n2AD15C6037F6A5BCB58995910338610A63E55F24CF1F75744341BCEA80BA6648EF9D466A6380DB\n92A642754522FA655B9CC02780B19EE69C975425328090EBC07415F3B6776BE74A482222161ED2\n18E78849318EE6A820120A0EA9C835CEA2528548B0F2733D6##>\n\n",
      "en": "# Command 130 - RSA Data Encrypt\n\n- **Command ID:** `130`\n- **Title:** RSA Data Encrypt\n- **PDF Pages:** 802 - 803\n\n---\n\n\n<!-- Page 802 -->\n\n130 - RSA Data Encrypt\nCommand 130 encrypts data using an RSA public key.\nReference Information: Section 9, “Asymmetric Cryptography.”\n| Key Type |  | Default Setting | MFK Type |\nAKB Disabled AES\nCommand\n\n```\n<130#Padding Scheme#Data#Public Key#>\n```\n\nResponse\n\n```\n<230#Encrypted Data#[Check Digits]#>\n```\n\n\n### Command description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Command identifier | 3 | 130 |\n| 1 | Padding Scheme | 1 | Indicates the padding algorithm used with RSA encryption: |\n0 = OAEP-SHA256 padding (both hashes = SHA256)\n2 = OAEP-SHA1 padding\n9 = PKCS#1 v1.5 padding\nLegal Characters: 0, 2, 9\n| 2 | Data | Variable | The data to be encrypted. The length of the data must be |\neven. The minimum length is two characters. The\nmaximum length depends on the padding scheme and size\nof the RSA modulus (SOM)\nField 1 Maximum length\n0 2*SOM - 132\n2 2*SOM - 84\n9 2*SOM - 22\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\n| 3 | Public Key | Variable | The Public Key to encrypt the data. |\nHeaders Supported: 3DRE*000, where * = E, N or S.\nWhen field 1 is 0 or 2: 3DRO*000 is also supported.\nThe length of this field depends upon the length of the\nPublic Key. This field can contain a key slot number where\nthe Public Key is stored in the RSA Key Table.\nLegal Characters: Printable ASCII\n\n\n<!-- Page 803 -->\n\n\n### Response description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 2 | 230 |\n| 1 | Encrypted Data | 2*SOM | The encrypted data. |\n| 2 | [Check Digits] | 0, 8 | The check digits of the RSA public key pair. The check |\ndigits are the leftmost 8 hexadecimal characters of the\nSHA256 of the RSA public key\nThis field will contain 8 hexadecimal characters only\nwhen field 3 of the command contains a number in the\nrange of 0 - 199.\nAKB AES MFK Example\nAES Master File Key = 2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF\nFEDCBA9876543210, check digits = B9BCAB7B62, see “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component\nvalues.\nThis command generates data which is returned in the response, your responses will not exactly match the\nresponses shown below.\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<130#0#8F#3DREE000,0001030100D80E983004D54EEF3EF15D103972EB9282BE14CF6CCC44D0A\n```\n\nCED6B6E6A2C9C976582F0870D7AA35C1E1F8DDA8083C7D023A0156E71A388206AADD9F0B536FF4\nE6EF15EE468AFA0AF187791D79EA0A1AF30955735344553888F2DBCB6D13EBEEAF908A199783B5\n72D71C30519F0CCFB3E5735393790C1CAF6ADA6512749F72814718086CE6F151FA9DC77CA2A4F9\nD15726B679D0516F11F30A0E28FAC127747EE8A0686BD5A222DEF36FB38B393DF0E5988F682BFC\n5B6F1682726B623BD7F5AE2B8901D79E356B3CAB9B5936722701A5652B14C16EE4DC750715556A\n87AC4B97E9B266F316A3E9AFDCDFD79C8157C323653F1959BAE851817933D2D3DE26DFCA3,F088\n979B7C9F4AC8B6AA429F4FF48C5C#>\nThe HSM returns a response similar to this:\n\n```\n<230#14815159E757A287867ADF7F1F2161A86F4B39054BDAD687054B762F21BC9BAB097AFDA69\n```\n\n46BBF1C340BB2C989B1B969E623D2FBF87228E69FA317FFB42D5DD64FB794D095554A136DB5DFF\n90D9A13C79FBED8459132A736D2E55D4F9C3BC40CA1DAF54E7816D4D1A2F2742564DB8C76DD2BD\nF5E6BEDAB9FE2C0DF5B93848A7B04DB45D051BE8DF9E4BF1AE96FC30051A5F091D3670E0B86EF7\n2AD15C6037F6A5BCB58995910338610A63E55F24CF1F75744341BCEA80BA6648EF9D466A6380DB\n92A642754522FA655B9CC02780B19EE69C975425328090EBC07415F3B6776BE74A482222161ED2\n18E78849318EE6A820120A0EA9C835CEA2528548B0F2733D6##>\n\n"
