      "es": "# Command 14B - Generate an AS2805 Send KEKr Validation Response\n\n- **Command ID:** `14B`\n- **Title:** Generate an AS2805 Send KEKr Validation Response\n- **PDF Pages:** 903 - 904\n\n---\n\n\n<!-- Page 903 -->\n\n14B - Generate an AS2805 Send KEKr Validation\nResponse\nCommand 14B generates the AS2805.6.3 key confirmation response cryptogram from a received key\nconfirmation request cryptogram. This response is sent back to the original sender.\n| Key Type |  | Default Setting | MFK Type |\nAKB Disabled AES\nThe confirmation request message is created by generating a variable size random nonce (RN) and\nencrypting it with the appropriate AS2805 variant. The receiver decrypts the nonce, inverts it, and encrypts\nthe result with the response AS2805 variant. This command calculates that response message.\nTo maximize interoperability, this command does not check the parity of received value or adjust their\nparity. Command 14A generates parity-adjusted values.\nCommand\n| <14B#Key | Exchange   Key#Received |  | Request   Cryptogram##> |\nResponse\n\n```\n<24B#Response Cryptogram#Check Digits#>\n```\n\n\n### Command description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents   Length |  | Description |\n| 0 | Command identifier   3 |  | 14B |\n| 1 | Key Exchange Key   74 |  | The Key Exchange Key used to receive keys for node-to-node |\ncommunication; this must be a double-length or triple-length\n3DES key.\nHeaders Supported: 3KDD*00A, 3KDN*00A, where * =\nE, N, or S.\nLegal Characters: Printable ASCII\n| 2 | Received Request 16, 32, 48 |  | The received request cryptogram, eKEKrV1(RN). |\nCryptogram\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\n| 3 | Reserved   0 |  | This field must be empty. |\n\n\n<!-- Page 904 -->\n\n\n### Response description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents   Length |  | Description |\n| 0 | Response Identifier   3 |  | 24B |\n| 1 | Response 16, 32, 48 |  | The key confirmation response cryptogram, or |\nCryptogram eKEKrV2(~RN).\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\n| 2 | Check Digits   6 |  | The KEKr check digits are the leftmost six hexadecimal |\ncharacters that result from encrypting zeros.\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\nUsage Notes\nV1 = 0x82828282828282828282828282828282\nV2 = 0x84848484848484848484848484848484\nAKB AES MFK Example\nAES Master File Key = 2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF\nFEDCBA9876543210, check digits = B9BCAB7B62, see “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component\nvalues.\n16 character cryptogram\n Cleartext Key Exchange Key = 0123456789ABCDEFFEDCBA9876543210, check digits = 08D7B4\nThe Key Exchange Key in AKB format:\n3KDNN00A,EDEB3EAA4658F39EBDC157ADFEC2F588ACE94E1E4BC39918,D748CD751862831D\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<14B#3KDNN00A,EDEB3EAA4658F39EBDC157ADFEC2F588ACE94E1E4BC39918,D748CD751862831\n```\n\nD#D3E3F0F596317C9E##>\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<24B#A3F866E92AC0D243#08D7B4#>\n```\n\n\n",
