    "markdownDoc": {
      "es": "# Command 312 - Generate PAN Binding Token\n\n- **Command ID:** `312`\n- **Title:** Generate PAN Binding Token\n- **PDF Pages:** 1113 - 1114\n\n---\n\n\n<!-- Page 1113 -->\n\n312 - Generate PAN Binding Token\nCommand 312 generates a PAN binding token. PCI prohibits changing the PAN when translating or\nverifying a PIN block. This restriction creates a problem when the incoming PIN block was encrypted using\na tokenized PAN. This command solves the issue by creating a cryptographic binding between the \"real\"\nPAN and the tokenized value via the AES CMAC algorithm. The resulting blob is a PAN Binding token.\n| Key Type |  | Default Setting | MFK Type |\nAKB Disabled AES\nCommand\n\n```\n<312#Binding Version#Algorithm#PAN Binding Key#Tokenized PAN#PAN#>\n```\n\nResponse\n\n```\n<412#PAN Binding Token#Check Digits#>\n```\n\n\n### Command description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Command ID | 3 | 312 |\n| 1 | Binding Version | 1 | The format of the PAN binding token. |\nLegal Characters: 1\n| 2 | Algorithm | 1 | This field indicates the MAC algorithm to use: |\n2 = AES CMAC\nLegal Characters: 2\n| 3 | PAN Binding Key | 74, 90, | The CMAC Key encrypted under the MFK. |\n106, 110\nHeaders Supported: 3BJN*00B, 3BJG*00B, where * = E,\nN, or S.\nLegal Characters: Printable ASCII\n| 4 | Tokenized PAN | 12-19 | The tokenized PAN value. If only \"compact\" PIN blocks |\nare supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this can be\neither the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized card\nnumber. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full card\nnumber.\nLegal Characters: 0-9\n\n\n<!-- Page 1114 -->\n\n\n### Command description (Continued)\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 5 | PAN | 12-19 | The value of the actual PAN. If only \"compact\" PIN |\nblocks are supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this\ncan be either the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized\ncard number. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full\ncard number.\nLegal Characters: 0-9\n\n### Response description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 3 | 412 |\n| 1 | PAN Binding Token | 46-60 | The generated PAN Binding Token. |\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\n| 2 | Check Digits | 10 | The check digits are the leftmost ten digits from CMAC |\nof a zero block.\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\nUsage Notes\n This command should be enabled in the issuing or tokenization environments rather than an on-line\nenvironment where PIN blocks are being processed. If the environment lacks sufficient compensating\ncontrols to limit the use of this command, this command can be enabled as a counted command.\n It is possible that a system may need to translate between two different tokenization schemes. While\nthis command is written assuming that the second PAN input is the actual user PAN, there is nothing in\nthe HSM architecture that prevents translating between two PAN tokens as long as the two are bound\ntogether.\nAKB AES MFK Example\nThe AES Master File Key is:\n2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF FEDCBA9876543210\ncheck digits = B9BCAB7B62. See “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component values.\n The clear text CMAC Key = 00001111222233334444555566667777, check digits = FEF900C20E\nThe CMAC Key in AKB format\n3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E087BF62\n Tokenized PAN = 1234567890123456789\n PAN = 9876543210987654\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<312#1#2#3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E08\n```\n\n7BF62#1234567890123456789#9876543210987654#>\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<412#01191234567890123456789169876543210987654FB6719C26E4FF844B#FEF900C20E#>\n```\n\n\n",
      "en": "# Command 312 - Generate PAN Binding Token\n\n- **Command ID:** `312`\n- **Title:** Generate PAN Binding Token\n- **PDF Pages:** 1113 - 1114\n\n---\n\n\n<!-- Page 1113 -->\n\n312 - Generate PAN Binding Token\nCommand 312 generates a PAN binding token. PCI prohibits changing the PAN when translating or\nverifying a PIN block. This restriction creates a problem when the incoming PIN block was encrypted using\na tokenized PAN. This command solves the issue by creating a cryptographic binding between the \"real\"\nPAN and the tokenized value via the AES CMAC algorithm. The resulting blob is a PAN Binding token.\n| Key Type |  | Default Setting | MFK Type |\nAKB Disabled AES\nCommand\n\n```\n<312#Binding Version#Algorithm#PAN Binding Key#Tokenized PAN#PAN#>\n```\n\nResponse\n\n```\n<412#PAN Binding Token#Check Digits#>\n```\n\n\n### Command description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Command ID | 3 | 312 |\n| 1 | Binding Version | 1 | The format of the PAN binding token. |\nLegal Characters: 1\n| 2 | Algorithm | 1 | This field indicates the MAC algorithm to use: |\n2 = AES CMAC\nLegal Characters: 2\n| 3 | PAN Binding Key | 74, 90, | The CMAC Key encrypted under the MFK. |\n106, 110\nHeaders Supported: 3BJN*00B, 3BJG*00B, where * = E,\nN, or S.\nLegal Characters: Printable ASCII\n| 4 | Tokenized PAN | 12-19 | The tokenized PAN value. If only \"compact\" PIN blocks |\nare supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this can be\neither the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized card\nnumber. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full card\nnumber.\nLegal Characters: 0-9\n\n\n<!-- Page 1114 -->\n\n\n### Command description (Continued)\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 5 | PAN | 12-19 | The value of the actual PAN. If only \"compact\" PIN |\nblocks are supported (e.g. ISO-0 and ISO-3), then this\ncan be either the 12 digit ANSI PAN or the full tokenized\ncard number. If ISO-4 may be supported, include the full\ncard number.\nLegal Characters: 0-9\n\n### Response description\n\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Field | Contents | Length | Description |\n| 0 | Response identifier | 3 | 412 |\n| 1 | PAN Binding Token | 46-60 | The generated PAN Binding Token. |\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\n| 2 | Check Digits | 10 | The check digits are the leftmost ten digits from CMAC |\nof a zero block.\nLegal Characters: 0-9, A-F\nUsage Notes\n This command should be enabled in the issuing or tokenization environments rather than an on-line\nenvironment where PIN blocks are being processed. If the environment lacks sufficient compensating\ncontrols to limit the use of this command, this command can be enabled as a counted command.\n It is possible that a system may need to translate between two different tokenization schemes. While\nthis command is written assuming that the second PAN input is the actual user PAN, there is nothing in\nthe HSM architecture that prevents translating between two PAN tokens as long as the two are bound\ntogether.\nAKB AES MFK Example\nThe AES Master File Key is:\n2ABC3DEF45670189 98107645FED3CBA2 0123456789ABCDEF FEDCBA9876543210\ncheck digits = B9BCAB7B62. See “AES Key (256-bit)” on page 1374 for component values.\n The clear text CMAC Key = 00001111222233334444555566667777, check digits = FEF900C20E\nThe CMAC Key in AKB format\n3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E087BF62\n Tokenized PAN = 1234567890123456789\n PAN = 9876543210987654\nThe command looks like this:\n\n```\n<312#1#2#3BJNE00B,2555E8880E29821FC38300C621DBBD39,DA8E3CD9A847FFC0CFD438A1E08\n```\n\n7BF62#1234567890123456789#9876543210987654#>\nThe HSM returns this response:\n\n```\n<412#01191234567890123456789169876543210987654FB6719C26E4FF844B#FEF900C20E#>\n```\n\n\n"
    },
